WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.909
- [Dr. Mike] Wait, wait
I wanna get a good shot.

00:00:01.909 --> 00:00:03.530
Okay, go, go.
- [Yvette] You ready?

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:04.750
One, two, three.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.000
- All right, it's a bright and early day.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.728
It's time to be a doctor.

00:00:11.728 --> 00:00:14.234
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:17.036
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.036 --> 00:00:19.160
He has such an aggressive tongue.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.840
All right, let's take this
little guy for a walk.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:21.673
You ready?

00:00:21.673 --> 00:00:22.598
Let's go.

00:00:22.598 --> 00:00:25.603
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:28.370
Look at that, peeing right on the grate.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.300
Did not make a mess.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.230
I trained him well.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.560
You can't see the Empire State Building.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.393
It's right there.

00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:34.510
Touch it.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:35.343
Touch it.

00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:37.840
As any veteran dog owner will tell you,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.850
one of the biggest
struggles of having a pup

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.780
is when you're walking them in the morning

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.827
and you wanna get to work

00:00:44.827 --> 00:00:47.090
and your dog refuses to poop.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.232
Bear, go poop.

00:00:48.232 --> 00:00:50.660
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.574
My first patient today
is actually at 8:00 a.m.

00:00:53.574 --> 00:00:55.040
but I wanna get in a little earlier

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.730
to start working on my
notes from the other day.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.090
Notes are the bane of
the existence of a doctor

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.570
because not only do we
wanna see the patients,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.440
spend time with them,
but then we become slaves

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.910
to the electronic medical record.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:07.860
It's pretty brutal.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.873
What is that?

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.872
Bear, you wanna put that away?

00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:15.853
All right, let's get to work.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.310
Today I'm gonna be working
a long 12, 14 hour shift

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.230
as an outpatient family medicine doctor.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.020
Now what does outpatient mean?

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.700
It means that I'm gonna be seeing patients

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.110
in an office setting where I don't have

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.710
to admit them, meaning that

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.900
they are not gonna stay
overnight in the hospital.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.370
So it's basically when you
go in for your doctor's visit

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.130
for a check-up, for a problem,

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.730
that's what I'm gonna be doing today.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.020
That's primarily actually what I do.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.480
So today we'll probably
have around 30 patients

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.100
where maybe after each visit I'll come

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.330
and tell you what I did

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.901
and we can have a little
learning opportunity.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:55.988
(tone dinging)
(banging)

00:01:55.988 --> 00:01:57.180
Oh my God.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.110
Major fail.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.750
When I see patients in my office,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.680
I see them for anything.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.330
Acute problems, meaning
like they get sick today

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:05.660
or they have pain today

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.910
or something's going on
where it's an urgent matter,

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:11.310
but I also see them for primary care,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.490
meaning that they're coming in

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:15.340
for chronic disease
management and counseling.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.430
So I have diabetic patients that need

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.470
to check their numbers,

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.880
make sure they're taking
their medications optimally.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.830
It's a really fun job

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.960
'cause you really never
know what's gonna happen.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.204
You don't know what kind
of patient's gonna walk in.

00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:28.360
(tone dinging)
(banging)

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.950
God if you guys didn't know any better

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.940
you would have though
I got into an accident.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.440
My commute isn't that bad.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.010
It's around 45 minutes.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.200
It's a reverse commute.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.360
Some people are like, wait,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.820
you work in New Jersey
but live in New York?

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:42.653
Yeah.

00:02:42.653 --> 00:02:43.760
That's actually a great strategy

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.150
because I do a lot of TV stuff in New York

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.560
so I have to be here,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.460
but then I love working in New Jersey

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.070
'cause that's where I did
my residency training,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.310
and I built up this
patient panel of patients

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.630
I actually love and
have a connection with.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.780
I'm growing old with them.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:02:59.950
It's kinda funny to say.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.529
I think we've got some great content.

00:03:01.529 --> 00:03:02.618
(tone dinging)
(banging)

00:03:02.618 --> 00:03:03.451
God.

00:03:03.451 --> 00:03:06.450
I wonder what kind of clip I need to buy

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.533
to keep this in my AC vent.

00:03:09.533 --> 00:03:10.500
(tone dinging)
(banging)

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.320
By the way, now that we're
just hanging out here,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.870
I'm having some water
and I'm driving to work,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:15.703
I need you to hit

00:03:15.703 --> 00:03:16.590
that subscribe button

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.070
and that notification bell

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.840
and set your alerts to all

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.960
just so that YouTube
algorithm doesn't mess with us

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:22.800
any further.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.290
I know only 15% of you
have done that so far.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.441
Step up your game.

00:03:26.441 --> 00:03:28.440
You can do better.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.241
Hit that notification bell.

00:03:30.241 --> 00:03:32.950
(engine revving)

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.200
I'm gonna get in trouble.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:40.119
Who's excited for work?
- Michael, how are you?

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:40.952
Good morning.

00:03:40.952 --> 00:03:42.231
- Are you excited for work Jenny?

00:03:42.231 --> 00:03:45.120
- Oh I'm so excited to see your face.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.630
- Isn't it a great day to save lives?

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.090
I'm gonna sit on this garbage can here.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:49.923
- Don't sit on the garbage can.

00:03:49.923 --> 00:03:50.756
- No, I'm gonna sit

00:03:50.756 --> 00:03:51.589
on the garbage can.

00:03:51.589 --> 00:03:52.422
Don't tell me what to do.
- Oh Michael.

00:03:52.422 --> 00:03:53.460
- You're not my mom.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.390
Maybe my work mom.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.350
What's the craziest
thing you've ever seen?

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:57.810
- You're making me think back here.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:58.643
- Yeah.

00:03:58.643 --> 00:04:00.060
- People that have passed

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.220
and there's a smile appears
on their face within seconds

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.523
of them totally passing away.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.540
That's pretty amazing.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:08.760
- Any good stories Jenny?

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.860
Let's cheer people up.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.760
- Michael, the good
stories is there's lots

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.040
of men walking around with nice scrubs.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.650
- Ooh.
- That's the best stories.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:19.286
- Jenny's getting feisty today.

00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:21.268
- (laughs) Get out of here.

00:04:21.268 --> 00:04:22.708
- Let's talk about INR.

00:04:22.708 --> 00:04:23.854
- Okay, what about INR?

00:04:23.854 --> 00:04:24.687
Let's talk about it.

00:04:24.687 --> 00:04:25.520
- Not on the vlog.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.530
- Why not?
- Okay.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.840
- INR is when a patient comes in

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.350
for a test to see if they're
taking their coumadin properly

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.260
and appropriately, so let's check it out.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.940
So when you're looking at an INR,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.940
what I'm looking at is to figure out,

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.230
A, is the patient's dosage correct?

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.920
B, are they coming in
at the right intervals?

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.410
So if their levels are all over the place

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:48.640
and we can't get any stability,

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.060
we have to figure out why that's happening

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.860
and we have to check them more often.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.560
But if they're consistently
having good numbers,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:56.669
we can spread 'em out.

00:04:56.669 --> 00:04:58.810
- Hey, hey hey hey hey.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:04:59.670
- Represent.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.130
- Represent, yes.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.523
- Jose, what's up baby.
- (laughs) (mumbles)

00:05:04.523 --> 00:05:05.920
Where'd you get this from?

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.206
- Today's a hospital vlog day, baby.

00:05:08.206 --> 00:05:09.039
- Woooo.

00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:09.872
- You ready for it?

00:05:09.872 --> 00:05:10.705
- I'm ready, I'm ready.
- You gonna try

00:05:10.705 --> 00:05:11.700
and get Liddy?

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.310
- I'm ready, I'm ready to get Liddy.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.466
- Yo, you gotta DJ it up.

00:05:14.466 --> 00:05:15.769
- (laughs) Whatcha wanna hear?
DJ-ing

00:05:15.769 --> 00:05:16.645
for our patients.
- Whatcha wanna hear?

00:05:16.645 --> 00:05:17.478
Whatcha wanna hear?

00:05:17.478 --> 00:05:18.910
- I wanna hear Reggaeton.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:19.753
- All right, I got you.

00:05:19.753 --> 00:05:21.051
- (mumbles) Reggaeton.
- Oh my god.

00:05:21.051 --> 00:05:21.884
What is going on in here?
- [Jose] Them bones them bones

00:05:21.884 --> 00:05:23.706
- Come on, come on it's a party.

00:05:23.706 --> 00:05:25.260
(laughing)

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.360
All right Jose, how many patients
you checking in right now?

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.440
You don't think you're working too hard.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.273
- I just got in.

00:05:29.273 --> 00:05:30.140
- All right, go do your job.
- All right.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.460
- 'Cause I need to see my patients.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.560
So my typical day goes like this.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.500
I come in.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.280
I try and catch up on the computer

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.550
to call patients back,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.090
to look at results,

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.150
to write some notes that
I missed the previous day,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.340
and then I go in

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.750
and every 15, 20 minutes
I have a new patient.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:49.760
I'm in and out of the rooms.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:50.593
I'm in and out of the rooms,

00:05:50.593 --> 00:05:51.590
like I just came out of a room

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.670
with a patient who was complaining

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:54.480
about really bad allergies.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.313
That's really interesting

00:05:55.313 --> 00:05:58.330
'cause typical pollen season is now over

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:02.410
and now we're getting into
that wintertime allergy season

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.360
and sometimes if you're using
the same allergy medication

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.200
for too long you can
actually build up a tolerance

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.320
to it and you need to rotate them.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.690
So what we're doing for this patient is

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.720
we're changing up the allergy medications.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.490
We're also giving medications directly

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.830
for where the symptoms are present,

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.360
so you think about the nose, the eyes,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.500
and we're really trying to get them better

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.020
by thinking big picture
'cause it's not enough

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:25.040
to just think about prevention.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.030
We also need to think about
treating the active symptoms.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.420
Today's gonna be a busy day
but I'm excited to get started.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.220
So I just had to call a patient back

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:34.630
for his ultrasound.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.300
He came in with gynecomastia,

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.910
which is he had a breast mass,

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.410
basically breast tissue developing.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.100
And when we do that, we have
to check hormonal levels.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.100
If those hormonal levels are elevated,

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.850
then we gotta do an ultrasound.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.720
If the ultrasound is negative
then we gotta do a CAT scan.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.510
If that's negative then
we need to know something.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.980
So it's really interesting how we have

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.030
to follow these sets of guidelines

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:56.580
so that we don't do extra testing

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:57.640
and we don't do undertesting.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.810
It's a very fine line that
we have to follow here.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:00.650
What's going on?

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:01.483
- What's good?

00:07:01.483 --> 00:07:03.690
- You wanna tell the peeps
if you get any crazy calls

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.700
with people asking to come see me?

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:06.533
- Uh, yeah.

00:07:06.533 --> 00:07:10.720
Hi, can I speak to Dr. Mike?

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.330
Uh, no.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.040
Can I help you?

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.710
Yeah, but can I just talk to him?

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:18.980
Can I have his number?

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.398
No, no no.

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:21.930
We don't deal with that here, sorry.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.790
- So I'm with Dr. Stewart Green here.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:24.800
- Hello, hi.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.940
- Who is an expert in
everything when it comes

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:28.830
to mental health.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:29.663
- (laughs)

00:07:29.663 --> 00:07:30.530
- Taught me everything I know.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.900
I talk a lot about anxiety and depression

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.700
on my channel, how rates are going up.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.010
Is that something you've noticed?

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.750
- There is a lot more sort of tension,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.080
the large surrounding environment

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.910
of the country these days,

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.890
and those things of course
impact patients' lives

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.440
and people who are susceptible

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.880
to developing significant
anxiety and anxiety disorders,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:56.713
as we say.

00:07:56.713 --> 00:07:58.200
So yes, there's more of it,

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.130
and it's affecting youth as well.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:00.963
- All right.

00:08:00.963 --> 00:08:01.880
'Cause I got a really long day,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.470
I only get to eat once before I get home

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.340
which is gonna be around like 10:00 p.m.,

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.996
so I picked up some Chipotle.

00:08:07.996 --> 00:08:09.090
All right Tia.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:09.970
What are you gonna tell me?

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:13.650
You gonna tell about a public patient

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.790
who is here for an employee physical?

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:16.710
We do that here, right?

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:17.580
- Yes we do actually.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.100
- And what do we do
generally for those patients?

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.610
- We just make sure they can breathe

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:22.573
and they have a pulse.

00:08:22.573 --> 00:08:23.406
- What?

00:08:23.406 --> 00:08:25.507
What does a CMA do 'cause no
one could answer this question.

00:08:25.507 --> 00:08:26.340
- Oh, okay.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.550
Well generally it's a medical assistant

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.850
so I'm still able to, like
nurses back in the day,

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:33.050
bring patients in,

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:36.341
do vital signs, draw
blood, give injections,

00:08:36.341 --> 00:08:40.470
and assist with procedures
like skin biopsies,

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:41.410
toenail removal.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:43.026
- You gave the greatest answer so far.

00:08:43.026 --> 00:08:43.859
- No problem.

00:08:43.859 --> 00:08:44.692
- You're a rock star.

00:08:44.692 --> 00:08:45.525
- Yes.

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:48.200
- So I just had a pretty
interesting patient encounter.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.760
Patient's coming in for an earache.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:52.500
I looked through the patient's notes

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.090
and she's been here a lot,

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.100
and I see that she's been
diagnosed with cancer,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.340
was familiar to me so I
looked back in my notes

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:00.090
and I found that I saw this patient

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:02.820
about a year ago.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.020
And it was actually me
who caught the cancer.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:07.310
I didn't know this.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:10.000
When I walked into the room
the patient was basically

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.970
in tears because she was happy to see me

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:17.000
because I was the one that
initially got the x-ray

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.700
to find her cancer

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.120
because what happened
was she had pneumonia.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.380
They did an x-ray,

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.610
but then when you get a pneumonia,

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.090
the way it shows up on an x-ray

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.760
it can actually cover the cancer,

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.600
if there is a cancer or anything there.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.750
So that's why whenever
a person gets better

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.910
we ask them to come back
in for a follow-up x-ray

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.500
to make sure that there's
nothing behind the pneumonia.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:39.760
And what do you know,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.530
she was still a little bit coughing.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:42.363
Month went by.

00:09:42.363 --> 00:09:43.660
I said let's get another x-ray.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.320
Boom, there was something else there.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.000
I ended up getting a CAT
scan a few days later

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.410
and we found a cancer that was treated

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.980
and now she's doing great.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:51.960
But now for her to come in for an earache

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.600
and be doing well after
being diagnosed, treated,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.330
and her being really happy,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.880
she told me something really cute.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.250
She says, "My kids,
especially the little one,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:02.430
are such big fans of yours."

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:03.380
I said because of YouTube?

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.410
They're like no, because
you saved my life.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:07.020
And I said, oh how old is your little one?

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:08.350
She's like 39.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.220
We have one of our staff
members that's been here

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.240
for almost a decade and a half

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.490
so we're gonna go say good-bye to her,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:17.323
but she's not ready.

00:10:17.323 --> 00:10:18.581
It's a surprise.

00:10:18.581 --> 00:10:20.468
Hey!

00:10:20.468 --> 00:10:23.340
(clapping)

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:24.173
We love you.

00:10:24.173 --> 00:10:26.462
Cut the cake.
(cheering)

00:10:26.462 --> 00:10:27.457
Cut the cake.

00:10:27.457 --> 00:10:29.690
Yvette we're trying to teach everybody how

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.040
to be healthy and happy
and what are you doing?

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:34.374
What are you doing here?

00:10:34.374 --> 00:10:35.207
- You really have the (mumbles)

00:10:35.207 --> 00:10:36.455
to call me out after I
(mumbles) your cake--

00:10:36.455 --> 00:10:37.421
- I didn't eat cake.

00:10:37.421 --> 00:10:39.045
I didn't eat no cake.
- (laughs)

00:10:39.045 --> 00:10:40.820
- [Dr. Mike] Look at that, what is that?

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:41.653
- He had cake.

00:10:41.653 --> 00:10:42.486
I didn't have cake.

00:10:42.486 --> 00:10:43.319
- Cut the cake.

00:10:44.946 --> 00:10:46.920
Cut the cake.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.670
Can you tell I'm a little tired.

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:51.330
Yeah, it's past 8:00 p.m.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.970
We started this day at six.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:54.640
I'm almost there, the tail end.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.220
Just so you understand the struggle

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.180
that a doctor has to go through,

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.470
like I've worked non-stop
pretty much all day

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:01.930
for these 12, 13 hours,

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:03.330
whatever I worked,

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.610
but my notes aren't all done.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:09.120
So I either have to sit at my
desk finishing these notes,

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.700
or I have to go home,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.560
do them at home or tomorrow
morning wake up extra early

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.730
before I start doing my other stuff

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:18.090
and do the notes.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:19.600
So as you can see,

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:23.130
burnout can start happening very soon

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.140
and very easily for physicians.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:29.250
So the struggle is real but
it's worthwhile struggle.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.230
Yvette, I'm scared.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.850
I don't wanna get the flu shot.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.130
Gives you the flu.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.440
It does all this bad stuff.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:37.520
I don't want it.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.100
- You better get it or
you gonna wear a mask

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:41.781
all flu season.

00:11:41.781 --> 00:11:43.850
Would you like some cold spray first?

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:45.480
- I don't, do I want cold spray?

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:47.430
No, I'm already cold as it is.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:49.520
- [Yvette] It will numb the area.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:50.800
- Oh my God.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:53.080
I wish I wore an undershirt
today for the video.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.193
She said no heavy lifting but why?

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.528
I wanna heavy lift.

00:11:58.528 --> 00:12:00.100
- No, no, no irritation

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.140
just in case
- Please.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.270
- You have some type of discomfort,

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.080
elevated temperature.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:06.850
You can take whatever you normally take.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.080
Drink as much fluid
- [Dr. Mike] It's happening.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.470
- as possible.
- [Dr. Mike] It's happening.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.360
- Take the pressure off a your arm.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.640
Let it hang free so if you
could just sit up a little bit.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:15.867
No pressure on your arm.
- [Dr. Mike] Oh my God.

00:12:15.867 --> 00:12:17.369
It's happening.
- Just let it hang.

00:12:17.369 --> 00:12:18.202
- [Dr. Mike] There's so much

00:12:18.202 --> 00:12:19.035
nudity here.
- [Yvette] Are you ready?

00:12:19.035 --> 00:12:20.750
- [Dr. Mike] Wait, wait
I wanna get a good shot.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.280
Okay, go, go.
- You ready?

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.340
One, two, three, 35.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:28.060
- Ooh, ooh, I'm just kidding.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:29.550
It doesn't hurt.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:30.520
- It actually doesn't--

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.500
- It actually feels good.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:34.580
It feels like a relief

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.340
because I'm no longer gonna get the flu.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.362
Well at least 40 to 60% of the time.

00:12:39.362 --> 00:12:41.985
- (laughs) 40 to 60.

00:12:41.985 --> 00:12:44.410
- I went to walk out of
the office without my tie.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:45.890
Straight Magic Mike style.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.140
This is what happens when
it's almost 10:00 p.m.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:50.350
Do you know where your
children are Yvette.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:51.183
- Yes.

00:12:51.183 --> 00:12:52.071
- Where?

00:12:52.071 --> 00:12:53.670
- Home and in school.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.218
- Margaret, do you know
where your child is?

00:12:56.218 --> 00:12:57.051
- [Staff] I don't have any.
- Not really.

00:12:57.051 --> 00:12:59.100
I know where one of them
is, but there's three.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.000
- Well at least you got 33%.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.920
Woo, long day in the office

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.680
but we saved some lives.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.050
Took care of business.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.590
Now I'm gonna head home,

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.710
walk and feed the Bear.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.550
Maybe do some more work at home,

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:14.730
but that's what a day in the
hospital's like boys and girls.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:15.713
Hope you enjoyed.

